{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: independently review the trust-assumption record at exact clean pushed head 802a7c31345129f7dfbeda447ec8ce57ced3e843 on request/env-trust-assumption. Checkout clean, origin matching, base is current main 3a5d952c, one commit ahead. Documentation only.\n\nWHAT THIS IS FOR. Hugh ruled at 57df8d25 that the operator's ambient Git environment is outside gitseq's threat model. Three findings on the remote-link lane turned on where that boundary sits, and each cost a round partly because it was written down nowhere — a reader of internal/app could see the environment being bounded and had no way to tell whether it was a defence, a determinism property, or habit.\n\nWHERE IT WENT. The Host posture section, right after the paragraph saying the supported posture is one trusted operator account and that malicious same-account processes are outside the resident's protection. There is no separate threat-model page; I looked before choosing.\n\nTHREE THINGS I WOULD LIKE JUDGED RATHER THAN SKIMMED.\n\nWhether the assumption as written matches the ruling exactly. I have listed the variable families and said the environment is trusted and that defending a hostile operator-controlled environment is a non-goal. If I have widened or narrowed it, that is the defect this lane has produced eight times and I would rather you catch a ninth than not.\n\nWhether the reason I gave is sound. I wrote that Git configuration names programs Git runs, so anyone who can set these variables can already run code as the operator, which is the account the resident's keys live in — so a defence here protects nothing such an attacker could not reach otherwise. That is my reconstruction of why the ruling is right, not something hugh said. If it is wrong the page should carry the rule without it.\n\nWhether the description of the existing code is accurate rather than flattering. I say internal/app and hermeticGitEnvironment bound the environment for determinism rather than defence. I believe that of internal/app because I wrote it and it is what the artifact claims. I am less certain of hermeticGitEnvironment, which predates all of this and whose author's intent I am inferring from what it does.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO. No code changed. The ruling explicitly declines the strip, so internal/gitstore and internal/app are untouched and hermeticGitEnvironment's behaviour is described, not annotated or weakened.\n\nGATES, run by me at this exact head from a run I watched exit. go test ./... -count=1 exit 0: 35 ok and 1 with no test files, zero failures and zero cached, internal/docset uncached at 79.203s. gofmt -l . empty; go build, go vet and git diff --check all exit 0.\n\nDo not merge.","body":{"branch":"request/env-trust-assumption","conditions":"Independent review of exact clean pushed head 802a7c31345129f7dfbeda447ec8ce57ced3e843 on request/env-trust-assumption, citing artifact babc61e4 at docs/reference/architecture.md. Governing request is planner's docs-only child 2996e429; my promise is dbe53373. Documentation only; one commit ahead of current main 3a5d952c. Record Architecture, Security and Simplification conclusions and file the verdict with gs review resting on that artifact. Judge whether the assumption as written matches hugh's ruling 57df8d25 exactly, whether the stated reason is sound, and whether the description of what internal/app and hermeticGitEnvironment are doing is accurate rather than flattering. Do not merge.","head":"802a7c31345129f7dfbeda447ec8ce57ced3e843","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}